summit later this month  uh let's analyze a little bit what's  been happening here we can bring in sami  puri who's author of russia's road to  war with ukraine joins us live now from  singapore so something i think you've  been listening into what's been said um  so vladimir putin's saying that russia  is coping with what he called this  the financial aggression the sanctions  just how stretched would you say russia  has been  by the sanctions put on it because of  its invasion of  ukraine yeah i mean it's uh quite a  surreal thing to watch vladimir putin  standing in vladivostok which is about  as far as you can get in russia away  from ukraine  now i think the stress that russia has  faced has been intense its economy of  course is now more cut off from the  western from many global markets than at  any point since the end of the cold war  and in some respects the way that the  war in ukraine has gone in russian uh  russia's subjection to sanctions means  that russia's economic fate is sealed it  has to become an asia facing eastern  facing economic power or it will  economically perish and that i think  means that this eastern economic forum  speak by putin is especially significant  and it's also significant because uh he  talks about a multi-polar world but  there aren't that many other leaders of  his level attending in person so i think  you know russia's uh economic edifice is  is i think a very precarious stage i  think is what i say  right  as i said the  chinese number three i believe is there  lee zhanchu head of the country's  legislature  due to have talked with putin a little  bit later could set up further talks  with the chinese premier down the line  later this month  clearly that relationship with china is  is critical for russia  it's absolutely critical and in fact uh  russia chinese bilateral trade has been  rising in previous years that's  accelerated by uh the conditions around  the ukraine war  and xi jinping and putin will uh will  meet eventually in uh in the g20 summit  in indonesia which is in november so if  they're able to meet bilaterally as  you're saying in advance of that uh  scheduled meeting in november that would  be very significant  i suppose the next question is where  would such meeting take place would  president xi come to russia would putin  travel out to china that in itself will  be very significant but this is yet to  be confirmed but i do think it's  important that the chinese set their  number three and not a more senior  official to this particular uh forum  right so if china aren't on that if  china is critical to russia how critical  is russia to china  well uh everyone will remember that  jinping and putin met at the beijing  winter olympics on the 4th of february  this year just before russia began this  invasion and they spoke of a deep and  enduring friendship they should have  documents around this  but in the seven or eight months since  then russia has probably become  something of a bit of an embarrassment  to china in the sense that the war in  ukraine has not gone as well as uh  russia almost certainly would have voted  and wanted to but nevertheless uh china  and russia do find each other to be  extraordinarily important because both  countries are experiencing different  degrees of sanctions from the usa and  from the west so in some respects they  have to band together but it is a bit of  a marriage of convenience between the  two of them of course there is the  obvious point to have a huge land border  and one final point on that which i  think this forum really speaks to is the  infrastructure development between china  and russia for example the power of  siberia pipeline and all sorts of  associated projects with modernization  of vladivostok as a port its ability to  take in cargo all the things we're  hearing about air travel in this region  obviously russia is going to put a lot  of focus now on the development of the  eastern side of the russian territory so  its trading potential with china can  actually match the the sort of the  wanted ambition to i think double  chinese russian bilateral trade which  both president xi and putin have spoken  about as an ambition to their countries  and one final thought samir putin also  talked about the possibilities of of new  markets and relationships the  possibilities of entering or at least  expanding the markets entering iranian  and middle eastern markets what about  that  hugely important and i think one of the  critical features of of the last six  seven months are the countries that  haven't sanctioned russia because they  are keeping russia afloat and they are i  think speaking to this grand vision that  putin presents in this speech of a path  to a multipolar world iran is critical  turkey as well uh putin mentioned turkey  with the great deals critical  but if we look at the attendance of this  war i think it's armenia mongolia  myanmar in terms of in-person attendance  by prime ministers i think russia has  got quite a long way to go to securing  uh its sort of return to  prominence in the international stage  given what's happened over the course of  2022  fantastic thanks for that analysis and  perspective i do appreciate that that's  samir puri speaking to us from singapore  as president vladimir putin addresses  the seventh eastern economic forum in  vladivostok in eastern  russia now

